    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "Prove that $\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational.\n\nIt satisfies $P(x)=x^4-10x^2+1=0$. The only possible rational roots are $\\pm1$, and $P(1)=P(-1)=-8\\ne0$.",
      "worked": "Let $a=\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{3}$.\n$$\\begin{align*}a^2&=(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{3})^2\\\\a^2&=2+2\\sqrt{6}+3\\\\a^2&=5+2\\sqrt{6}\\\\a^2-5&=2\\sqrt{6}\\\\(a^2-5)^2&=24\\\\a^4-10a^2+25&=24\\\\a^4-10a^2+1&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $a$ is a root of the monic polynomial $P(x)=x^4-10x^2+1$, which has integer coefficients. By the Integer Root Theorem, any rational root must be a divisor of $1$, so the only possibilities are $1$ and $-1$.\n$$\\begin{align*}P(1)&=1^4-10(1)^2+1\\\\P(1)&=-8\\ne0,\\\\P(-1)&=(-1)^4-10(-1)^2+1\\\\P(-1)&=-8\\ne0.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore $P$ has no rational roots, so $\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 85
  },
